[b]Trade Permission Engine[/b]

[b]What it does[/b]

Answers "does the market currently permit trading Long or Short here" — not "how probable is this specific setup". It's the successor to Directional Probability Engine: continuation and reversal are not two separate detectors, they're two readings of the same retracement ratio against the same confirmed reference leg.

[b]The key idea[/b]

Take the most recently confirmed leg (bullish low-to-high, or bearish high-to-low) and measure how deep the current counter-move has retraced against it.

[list]
[*][b]Continuation Maturity[/b] — a bell curve on that retracement centered near a typical pullback depth. Bet: the reference leg's own direction resumes.
[*][b]Reversal Maturity[/b] — the larger of a structural-failure bell curve near a full round-trip and an endpoint reading of the leg's own overextension. Bet: the reference leg is invalidated and a fresh opposite leg is starting.
[/list]

A bullish reference leg feeds Long Continuation AND Short Reversal. A bearish reference leg feeds Short Continuation AND Long Reversal — always mirrored, never a hardcoded direction.

[b]Live Trend Quality[/b]

A second, continuously-updated quality read alongside the quality frozen the instant the reference leg formed. Answers "is this impulse still healthy", not just "was it good when it formed" — a recency-weighted read across the last few completed legs' efficiency and range, so a strong leg from several swings ago can no longer single-handedly carry a Permission reading once the trend has gotten choppier.

[b]Permission Decay[/b]

Permission fades over bars with no fresh extreme, no accelerating momentum and no ATR expansion in the relevant direction, instead of sitting at a stale high value once the move that earned it has gone quiet.

[b]What feeds Permission[/b]

[list]
[*][b]Reference-leg quality[/b] — efficiency and overlap accumulated bar-by-bar and frozen the instant a pivot confirms.
[*][b]Live Trend Quality[/b] — see above.
[*][b]Maturity[/b] — the price bell curve above, multiplied by a time-maturity factor.
[*][b]Exhaustion[/b] — counter-move deterioration feeds Continuation; reference-impulse divergence at the leg's own endpoint feeds Reversal.
[/list]

These combine as a floored-multiplicative gate, not a weighted average — a strong single factor alone can no longer carry the score when the others haven't developed.

[b]What feeds the Trigger[/b]

An explicit state machine (Untriggered → Broken → Confirmed → Accepted), entered by either a same-bar rejection at the extreme (a marginal new high/low that closes back into range — the only genuinely leading ingredient) or a micro-swing structure break.

[b]Target and Invalidation[/b]

Frozen the instant a Trigger confirms and drawn directly on the price chart:

[list]
[*][b]T1[/b] — the nearest opposite-type confirmed pivot from a real pivot-history search, validated against a minimum distance (falls back to an ATR projection if none qualify)
[*][b]T2[/b] (optional) — scenario-aware, discarded unless it lies genuinely beyond T1
[*][b]Invalidation[/b] — scenario-aware, using the counter-move's own bounce range and retest depth
[/list]

A reward:risk feasibility factor and the decay factor both gate the final Active Permission score; same-bar Target/Invalidation conflicts resolve conservatively (invalidation first); a fresh opposite-direction trigger cancels the other side's still-open setup.

[b]Position Health (Long/Short)[/b]

A second, fully decoupled score for a different question: not "is a new entry attractive here", but "assuming I'm already Long (or Short), is that position still structurally healthy — hold, protect, reduce or exit?". It never reads CRV, Trigger state or the trade-lifecycle machinery, and combines its six components as a weighted average rather than the floored-multiplicative gate used for Permission, so one weak dimension can't crash a hold decision the way it's meant to crash a new-entry score.

[list]
[*][b]Live Quality[/b] — a genuinely continuous quality read, reacting within an in-progress pullback instead of only at the next confirmed leg
[*][b]Pullback Health[/b] — depth, duration and exhaustion of the current correction, scored so "no pullback yet, still making highs" reads as healthy rather than unready
[*][b]Trend Progress[/b] — has price actually broken beyond the last confirmed swing point, with recency decay
[*][b]Trend Fatigue[/b] — are recent legs shrinking
[*][b]Structure Intact[/b] — a graded ramp toward the same threshold that governs whether the reference leg itself flips — an ordinary pullback doesn't erode this
[*][b]Regime/Sideways-Risk[/b] — net movement vs. total path length, independent of leg structure
[/list]

Classified into four states: HOLD, PROTECT, REDUCE, EXIT — PROTECT fills the gap between "fine" and "get out" for a healthy-but-cooling trend.

[b]Display[/b]

No table. Color-coded histogram (light = Permission, solid = Active Permission), a thicker Permission line, a velocity line, a rising/falling confidence-band background, and two state-colored Position Health lines. Every per-role score is available in the data window.

[b]Important[/b]

These are rule-based 0-100 evidence scores, not statistically calibrated hit-rate probabilities. A separate outcome/backtest model would be required to translate a score into an empirical hit rate.

